  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&#x2019; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">By June 2021, the Samoa Worker Congress (SWC) supported over 200 workers who lost jobs due to the COVID-19 pandemic to find alternative employment through extended services provided to membership. The extended services included, continued career counselling trainings for mainly youth groups from the islands of Savaii and Upolu, registration with Job Seekers Registry and Informal Trainings provided by Samoa Qualification Authority and training on micro and small business start-ups.  

The SWC was also able to participate and coordinate inputs to the Samoa tripartite council on carrying out a Rapid Assessment (RA) on the impact of COVID-19 on employment, business. The submitted recommendations were endorsed by the Cabinet of the Government of Samoa. 

Based on the rapid assessment findings, SWC presented their specific recommendations to the Samoa employer groups to agree on bipartite recovery priorities, including priorities for the review of the Samoa DWCP and National Employment Policy. The ILO, initially through RBSA and RBTC funding, supported SWC on the following:

- technical advice through concept note designs on employment services and training;
- the methodology and outreach strategies to the informal economy;
- planning and integrating SDG and Youth concerns in working out of informality; and
- digital tools development for outreach to workers in the informal economy and discussions through online means.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.2.1. Number of national workers&#x2019; organizations with innovative strategies to attract new groups of workers and/or to improve their services</narrative>
